WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:07.120
Malaria strikes when parasites are transmitted
into the body, through the bites of infected

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.600
mosquitoes.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.350
With about 200 million cases worldwide, and
more than 400-thousand deaths each year, malaria

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.320
is a major burden on global health.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.670
The current standard method for malaria diagnosis,
in the field, is light microscopy of blood

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.800
films which involves the manual counting of
parasites.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.519
About 170 million blood films are examined
every year for malaria.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:36.340
Accurate parasite counts are essential to
diagnosing malaria correctly, testing for

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:42.300
drug-resistance, measuring drug-effectiveness,
and classifying disease severity.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.129
However, microscopic diagnostics is not standardized

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.120
and depends heavily upon the experience and skill

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:53.820
of the person peering through the microscope.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.539
To improve malaria diagnostics, the National
Library of Medicine, and our collaborators,

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:04.680
are developing Malaria Screener a fully-automated
system for parasite detection and counting

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.039
in blood films.

00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:12.110
Malaria Screener is a software application
that runs on a common smartphone utilizing

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.349
the phone's camera, to conduct automated malaria
screening in the field.

00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:22.191
An adapter connects the phone to the eyepiece
of a microscope allowing Malaria Screener

00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:28.830
to automatically analyze the blood film images
captured by the smartphone camera.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.750
Automatic parasite counting has several advantages
compared to manual counting:

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.720
It provides a more reliable and standardized
interpretation of blood films.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.110
It allows more patients to be served by reducing
the workload of the malaria field workers.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.080
And, it reduces diagnostic costs.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.810
To count parasites automatically, the system
uses image processing methods, to find parasites

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.470
in digitized images of blood films.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:01.410
The system first learns the typical shape
and visual appearance of parasites based upon

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.630
manually-annotated training images.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:10.119
Machine learning methods then detect the presence
of parasites perform the counting and discriminate

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:14.750
between parasite-infected and non-infected
cells.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.730
The system can process digital images acquired
on standard, light microscopy equipment, which

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.680
makes it well-suited for resource-poor settings.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.630
We are seeking partners to work with us to
demonstrate and evaluate Malaria Screener's

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.840
efficacy in the field.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.590
We hope that it will develop into an effective
tool to fight malaria throughout the world.

